ept nonce. Since the payload is identical, the expected output would be the same for all three. But we don't have expected outputs. However, the task description says \"The attached public fixtures define input shapes only.\" So we must design solve based on typical interpretation: apply passes sequentially, each pass replaces all occurrences of old with new in the current text. Then repeat rounds until no changes in a full round or max_rounds reached. The applications list should record the names of passes that actually made a replacement in a given round? Or just the sequence of all passes applied? Let's think: The fixture passes are: halve-a (aa->a), drop-b (bb->b), drop-c ( cc->\"\"). Starting text \"aaaa bb cc\". One round: apply halve-a: \"aa\" -> \"a\" twice? \"aaaa\" becomes \"aa\" (since two occurrences of \"aa\"? Actually \"aaaa\" contains overlapping? Usually replacement is non-overlapping, leftmost. \"aaaa\": replace \"aa\" with \"a\" gives \"aa\". Then apply drop-b: \"bb\" -> \"b\" gives \"aa b cc\"? Actually after first pass text is \"aa bb cc\"? Wait careful: original \"aaaa bb cc\". Apply halve-a: Replace all \"aa\" with \"a\". \"aaaa\" becomes \"aa\". So text becomes \"aa bb cc\". Apply drop-b: Replace \"bb\" with \"b\". \"bb\" becomes \"b\", so text becomes \"aa b cc\". Apply drop-c: Replace \" cc\" with \"\". So \" cc\" becomes \"\", text becomes \"aa b\". End of round 1. Now text is \"aa b\". Check if any change occurred in round 1? Yes. Round 2: Apply halve-a: \"aa\" -> \"a\", text becomes \"a b\". Apply drop-b: \"bb\"? Not present. Apply drop-c: \" cc\"? Not present. No change? Actually drop-b and drop-c did nothing. So after round 2, text \"a b\". Check if any change? Yes, halve-a changed. Round 3: Apply halve-a: \"aa\" not present, so no change. drop-b: \"bb\" not present. drop-c: \" cc\" not present. So no changes. Fixed point reached. Max_rounds=8, so stop at round 3. Applications list: Should we list all passes that were applied in the process? Or only the passes that actually caused a replacement? Or the sequence of passes in order of application? The output schema says \"applications\" as array of strings. Likely it's the list of names of passes that were applied (i.e., that caused a replacement) in the order they were applied. In the above, round 1: halve-a, drop-b, drop-c (all three caused changes). Round 2: halve-a caused change, drop-b and drop-c did not. Round 3: none. So total applications: [\"halve-a\", \"drop-b\", \"drop-c\", \"halve-a\"]. That would be 4 applications. Fixed_point: true because after round 3 no changes. Text: \"a b\". But note: original text had spaces: \"aaaa bb cc\". After transformations, final text \"a b\". That seems plausible.\n\nAlternatively, maybe applications counts each successful replacement per pass? But the names are unique per pass. Probably we record the name each time it is applied and makes a change. Some tasks record the sequence of passes that were applied. Let's assume that.\n\nAnother interpretation: apply all passes in each round, and for each pass if it makes any replacement, record its name once per round? That's what we did: we recorded name each time a pass caused at least one replacement in that round. That seems reasonable.\n\nBut we need to be careful: the passes might be applied in order given. Also, we should consider that replacements are greedy/non-overlapping? Typically, str.replace replaces all non-overlapping occurrences from left to right. That matches our simulation.\n\nAlso note: The max_rounds is given. If fixed point reached before max_rounds, we stop. If max_rounds reached but not fixed point, fixed_point should be false? Yes.\n\nNow we need to implement solve(case) that returns the JSON object with those three keys. We must use only standard library.\n\nImplementation plan:\n- Get text = case[\"text\"]\n- passes = case[\"passes\"] (list of dicts)\n- max_rounds = case[\"max_rounds\"]\n- applications = [] (list of strings)\n- for round in range(max_rounds):\n   changed_this_round = False\n   for p in passes:\n       old = p[\"old\"]\n       new = p[\"new\"]\n       name = p[\"name\"]\n       # Count replacements? We need to check if any replacement occurs.\n       # Use str.replace returns new string; compare length.\n       new_text = text.replace(old, new)\n       if new_text != text:\n           # At least one replacement occurred\n           applications.append(name)\n           changed_this_round = True\n           text = new_text\n       else:\n           pass # no change\n   if not changed_this_round:\n       fixed_point = True\n       break\nelse:\n   # loop completed without break\n   fixed_point = False\n\n- After loop, return {\"applications\": applications, \"fixed_point\": fixed_point, \"text\": text}\n\nBut careful: In the loop, we update text after each pass. That's correct because passes are applied sequentially within a round.\n\nEdge cases: If a pass causes multiple replacements, we still record the name once per round. That's what we do.\n\nNow we need to output a JSON object with key \"implementation\" containing the source code as a string. The source must define `solve(case)`. We need to ensure the source is valid Python and can be executed. We'll produce a single function.\n\nWe must escape newlines and quotes for JSON embedding. We'll write the source as a multi-line string inside the JSON.\n\nLet's write the solve function:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    text = case['text']\n    passes = case['passes']\n    max_rounds = case['max_rounds']\n    applications = []\n    for round in range(max_rounds):\n        changed = False\n        for p in passes:\n            old = p['old']\n            new = p['new']\n            new_text = text.replace(old, new)\n            if new_text != text:\n                applications.append(p['name'])\n                changed = True\n                text = new_text\n        if not changed:\n            return {'applications': applications, 'fixed_point': True, 'text': text}\n    return {'applications': applications, 'fixed_point': False, 'text': text}\n\nThat returns early if fixed point reached. Else after max_rounds, return fixed_point=False.\n\nBut we must ensure we don't mutate case. We don't; we only read.\n\nNow we need to convert this to a JSON string with proper escaping. The entire response must be a JSON object with key \"implementation\". So we'll construct that.\n\nWe'll write the Python code as a string, then embed it into a JSON object.\n\nWe need to be careful about indentation and quotes. We'll use triple quotes in the source? But then we need to escape those triple quotes? Better to use regular quotes and escape internal quotes. In the source, we use single quotes for keys, so no conflict with JSON string delimiters? The JSON string will be enclosed in double quotes, so any double quotes inside must be escaped. In our Python code, we use single quotes, so no double quotes except maybe in strings? None. So we can just write the code with single quotes. Then we need to escape newlines: replace each newline with \\n.\n\nAlternatively, we can use a raw string but in JSON we need escapes. We'll manually create the JSON.\n\nThe source will be:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    text = case['text']\n    passes = case['passes']\n    max_rounds = case['max_rounds']\n    applications = []\n    for round in range(max_rounds):\n        changed = False\n        for p in passes:\n            old = p['old']\n            new = p['new']\n            new_text = text.replace(old, new)\n            if new_text != text:\n                applications.append(p['name'])\n                changed = True\n                text = new_text\n        if not changed:\n            return {'applications': applications, 'fixed_point': True, 'text': text}\n    return {'applications': applications, 'fixed_point': False, 'text': text}\n\nNow convert to a one-line JSON string with escapes. We'll write a Python snippet to generate the JSON, but since we are writing manually, we need to ensure correctness.\n\nLet's break it: the string will contain newlines, which must be escaped as \\n. Backslashes? None. Quotes: only single quotes, so no escaping. So we can write the source as a multiline string and then replace newlines with \\n.\n\nWe'll do that in our mind: produce the JSON.\n\nThe entire response must be exactly one valid JSON object with key \"implementation\". No extra text.\n\nSo final output will be:\n\n{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    text = case['text']\\n    passes = case['passes']\\n    max_rounds = case['max_rounds']\\n    applications = []\\n    for round in range(max_rounds):\\n        changed = False\\n        for p in passes:\\n            old = p['old']\\n            new = p['new']\\n            new_text = text.replace(old, new)\\n            if new_text != text:\\n                applications.append(p['name'])\\n                changed = True\\n                text = new_text\\n        if not changed:\\n            return {'applications': applications, 'fixed_point': True, 'text': text}\\n    return {'applications': applications, 'fixed_point': False, 'text': text}\\n\"}\n\nBut note: The last line of the source ends with a newline? It's optional but fine. In the JSON string, the \\n at the end is okay.\n\nWe need to ensure that the source is valid Python and correctly indented. The indentation uses 4 spaces; in the JSON string, they are literal spaces.\n\nLet's double-check: The source string inside JSON must be enclosed in double quotes. All internal double quotes must be escaped. Our source has no double quotes, just single quotes. So okay.\n\nNow produce the final answer."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1037,"completion_tokens":2669,"total_tokens":3706,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1024},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":2489},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1024,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":13},"system_fingerprint